Description: Implements sample size planning for a future study from the summary
    statistics of a prior study, correcting the prior effect for publication bias
    and uncertainty so the planned study attains its target statistical power more
    reliably than methods that take a sample effect size at face value. Planners
    cover the one-sample, paired, independent, and Welch t tests; between-subjects
    analysis of variance and covariance; within-subjects and split-plot analysis
    of variance; a regression coefficient, the squared multiple correlation, and a
    subset of predictors; the Pearson correlation; a two-group multivariate
    comparison; and the chi square difference between nested models. The
    regression planners can treat the predictors as fixed or as sampled, the
    within-subjects and split-plot planners accept a sphericity correction, and
    each plan reports the effect size it is built on. A simulation function
    audits how often a plan attains its target power. The method is described in
    Anderson, Kelley, and Maxwell (2017) <doi:10.1177/0956797617723724>
    and extended to replication studies in Anderson and Kelley (2024)
    <doi:10.1037/met0000520>.
